.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt SYSLOGD 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm syslogd
.Nd log systems messages
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl CdFkNnTv
.Op Fl a Ar allowed_peer
.Op Fl b Ar address Ns Op : Ns Ar service
.Op Fl f Ar config_file
.Op Fl l Oo Ar mode : Oc Ns Ar path
.Op Fl m Ar mark_interval
.Op Fl O Ar format
.Op Fl P Ar pid_file
.Op Fl s
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon reads messages from programs on this host, from the kernel and from
other hosts, and writes each one to the files, terminals, programs, users
and hosts that
.Xr syslog.conf 5
selects for it.
.Pp
It reads from:
.Bl -bullet
.It
the local socket
.Pa /dev/log ,
a datagram socket in the
.Dv AF_UNIX
domain, to which
.Xr syslog 3
and
.Xr logger 1
send.
A stale file at that path is removed first, and the new socket is made mode
0666.
Further sockets are added with
.Fl l ;
.It
.Pa /dev/klog ,
the kernel message buffer
.Pq Xr klog 4 ,
which holds the kernel's messages from boot on until
.Nm
reads them;
.It
UDP port 514, unless
.Fl s ,
.Fl ss
or
.Fl N
is given.
.El
.Pp
The local sockets are created before
.Nm
goes into the background, so that
.Pa /dev/log
exists when the command returns.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl a Ar allowed_peer
Accept messages from the network only from
.Ar allowed_peer ,
which is
.Ar address Ns Oo / Ns Ar mask Oc Ns Op : Ns Ar service ,
with
.Ar mask
a prefix length or a dotted mask, or
.Ar domain Ns Op : Ns Ar service ,
where a
.Ar domain
of the form
.Li *. Ns Ar suffix
matches every name ending in
.Li \&. Ns Ar suffix .
Domain names are compared with the name the sender's address resolves to,
so under
.Fl n
they match nothing.
.Ar service
is a port number or
.Cm syslog .
May be repeated; without it every sender is accepted.
.It Fl b Ar address Ns Op : Ns Ar service
Receive on
.Ar address
instead of every address.
An empty
.Ar address ,
or
.Ql * ,
means every address.
May be repeated.
.It Fl C
Create a log file named in
.Xr syslog.conf 5
that does not exist, with mode 0600.
Without it, a missing file is reported and its rule is not carried out.
.It Fl d
Debug: stay in the foreground and also print
.Nm Ns 's
own messages, and rejected datagrams, on standard error.
.It Fl F
Stay in the foreground.
.It Fl f Ar config_file
Read
.Ar config_file
instead of
.Pa /etc/syslog.conf .
.It Fl k
Keep facility
.Cm kern
on messages that did not come from the kernel.
By default such messages are given facility
.Cm user .
.It Fl l Oo Ar mode : Oc Ns Ar path
Also receive on a local socket at
.Ar path ,
made mode
.Ar mode
(octal), 0666 by default.
May be repeated.
.It Fl m Ar mark_interval
Write a
.Dq -- MARK --
message of facility
.Cm mark ,
level
.Cm info ,
every
.Ar mark_interval
minutes, to the files selecting it that have not been written to for that
long.
The default is 20; 0 turns marks off.
.It Fl N
Open no network socket at all, for receiving or sending.
.It Fl n
Do not look up the names of hosts messages arrive from; use their addresses.
.It Fl O Ar format
Write messages as
.Cm bsd
or
.Cm rfc3164 ,
the default, or as
.Cm syslog
or
.Cm rfc5424 ,
both locally and to other hosts.
.It Fl P Ar pid_file
Write the process ID to
.Ar pid_file
instead of
.Pa /var/run/syslog.pid .
.It Fl s
Do not receive messages from the network.
Given twice, do not send messages to other hosts either.
The default
.Va syslogd_flags
in
.Xr rc.conf 5
is
.Fl s .
.It Fl T
Stamp messages from the network with the time they arrive, instead of the
time stamp they carry.
.It Fl v
Write each message's facility and level, as numbers, before its tag:
.Ql <4.6> .
Given twice, write them by name:
.Ql <auth.info> .
Only in the
.Cm rfc3164
format.
.El
.Ss Messages
A message is a
.Ql \&< Ns Ar priority Ns \&>
prefix, followed by an RFC 3164 or an RFC 5424 header and the text.
A message without a priority is
.Cm user.notice .
A newline or a NUL byte ends a message; control characters other than tab
are written as
.Ql ^X ,
and DEL as
.Ql ^? .
Messages longer than 8192 bytes are cut.
.Pp
Messages from this host, those from the local sockets and the kernel's, are
stamped with the time they arrive; the sender's own time stamp is ignored.
Messages from the network keep the time stamp they carry, unless
.Fl T
is given or they have none that can be read; an RFC 3164 time stamp, which
has no year, is given the current one, or the previous year for a month
well ahead of the current one.
.Pp
A message from the network is logged under the name of the host it came
from, as its address resolves, whatever host its header names.
.Pp
The kernel's lines are tagged
.Ql kernel .
A kernel line without a priority is
.Cm kern.notice .
.Pp
A message identical to the last one an action wrote, from the same host, is
not written again but counted.
The count is written as
.Dq last message repeated Ar N No times
when a different message arrives, or 30 seconds after the first repeat, and
after 2 and then 10 minutes while repeats go on.
.Ss Output
In the
.Cm rfc3164
format each line is
.Pp
.Dl Mmm dd hh:mm:ss host tag[pid]: text
.Pp
and in the
.Cm rfc5424
format
.Pp
.Dl <N>1 timestamp host app-name procid msgid structured-data text
.Pp
with the time stamp to the microsecond and its offset from UTC.
Times are local time.
Messages sent to other hosts over UDP are the same line, with the priority
prefix in the
.Cm rfc3164
format; they go out of the first receiving socket, so from port 514, or,
under
.Fl s ,
from a socket of their own.
.Pp
Messages to logged-in users, found in
.Xr utmpx 5 ,
are preceded by a
.Dq Message from syslogd@ Ns Ar host No at Ar time No ...
line.
Terminals are written without waiting; a terminal that is stopped loses the
message.
.Pp
A file that cannot be opened or written, and a regular expression that
does not compile, are reported once, as a message of facility
.Cm syslog ,
level
.Cm err ;
the rule is then left out until the configuration is read again.
A host that cannot be found is looked up again, at most once a minute.
A pipe command that exits is started again for the next message; messages
arriving meanwhile are lost.
.Pp
If the configuration file does not exist,
.Nm
logs
.Cm *.err
to
.Pa /dev/console
and
.Cm *.emerg
to every logged-in user.
.Nm Ns 's
own messages are of facility
.Cm syslog
and tagged
.Ql syslogd .
.Ss Signals
.Bl -tag -width SIGTERM
.It Dv SIGHUP
Read the configuration again, reopening every file and restarting every pipe
command.
.It Dv SIGTERM , Dv SIGINT
Log
.Dq exiting on signal Ar n ,
remove the local sockets and the process ID file, and exit.
.It Dv SIGPIPE
Ignored.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /var/run/syslog.pid -compact
.It Pa /etc/syslog.conf
The configuration.
.It Pa /etc/syslog.d
Further configuration, included by the default
.Pa /etc/syslog.conf .
.It Pa /var/run/syslog.pid
The process ID of the running
.Nm ,
which keeps the file locked with
.Xr flock 2 :
a second
.Nm
finds it locked and exits instead of taking
.Pa /dev/log .
.It Pa /dev/log
The local log socket.
.It Pa /dev/klog
The kernel message buffer.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr logger 1 ,
.Xr syslog 3 ,
.Xr klog 4 ,
.Xr unix 4 ,
.Xr newsyslog.conf 5 ,
.Xr syslog.conf 5 ,
.Xr dmesg 8 ,
.Xr newsyslog 8
.Sh STANDARDS
.Rs
.%A C. Lonvick
.%T The BSD syslog Protocol
.%R RFC 3164
.%D August 2001
.Re
.Pp
.Rs
.%A R. Gerhards
.%T The Syslog Protocol
.%R RFC 5424
.%D March 2009
.Re
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
daemon first appeared in
.Bx 4.3 .
This one is OxideBSD's own, written in Rust, with the options of
.Fx .
.Sh BUGS
Messages are sent and received over UDP only: the TCP and TLS actions
.Pq Li @@ Ns Ar host , Li @[ Ns Ar host Ns Li ]: Ns Ar port
and the
.Va tcp_*
and
.Va tls_*
options of
.Xr syslog.conf 5
are not supported yet.
There is no IPv6.
